+# The Daily Grind account preview
+
+A static, responsive homepage with an account dashboard. Open the site using its existing static hosting setup. No server process is needed for the checks below.
+
+## Included flows
+
+- A clearly marked Jamie Davis demo account, with separate demo actions that never claim to create a real credential.
+- New customer registration using a real, discoverable ES256 WebAuthn passkey.
+- Email and passkey sign-in, sign-out, and tab-scoped local sessions.
+- Add, rename, and remove passkeys; identity confirmation before account or credential changes; protection against removing the last usable credential.
+- Profile editing, email validation, duplicate email protection, and a saved email preference.
+- Accessible native dialogs, keyboard focus handling, cancellation, status messages, and responsive account navigation.
+
+## Local preview boundaries
+
+There is no authentication backend in this repository. Accounts, profile details, and public keys are stored in `localStorage`; the active account is stored in `sessionStorage`. Clearing site data deletes the local records. Device passkeys may remain in the operating system's credential manager and must be removed there separately.
+
+Real passkey creation and authentication use `navigator.credentials.create()` and `navigator.credentials.get()`. Authentication checks the challenge, origin, relying-party hash, user-presence and user-verification flags, optional user handle, and ECDSA signature. Challenges are generated with Web Crypto. The private key stays with the authenticator. HTTPS or localhost and a compatible browser/authenticator are required. Demo credentials are explicitly labeled and are never passed to WebAuthn.
+
+**This is not a production authentication system.** Browser storage and client-side sessions can be modified by a user or same-origin scripts. Before connecting customer data, move account ownership, expiring one-use challenges, registration attestation/credential verification, authentication verification, and authorization to a secure backend. Use server-managed secure HttpOnly sessions, credential uniqueness checks, signature-counter handling, rate limiting, account recovery, and a persistent database. Server enforcement must also cover profile changes and credential operations, including recent reauthentication and final-passkey protection. No production API is invented or silently called by this preview.
+
+The contact address is an illustrative `.example` address. Café information and ordering are informational placeholders. Fonts use Google Fonts, and the coffee photo uses Unsplash.
+
+WebAuthn reference: [MDN Web Authentication API](https://developer.mozilla.org/en-US/docs/Web/API/Web_Authentication_API).
+
+## Checks
+
+```sh
+node --check app.js
+node --test tests/webauthn.test.cjs
+```
+
+The security helper tests run in Node without a DOM, browser, or server. They cover challenge/origin/type checks, required authenticator flags, the relying-party hash, DER conversion, valid signature verification, and rejection of a modified signed payload. Native authenticator prompts require device testing before production deployment.
